Z||000000||Gokart||04-29-2000||05:21 AM||||I've only had the damn thing in a week and it's pooping out already <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> I thought maybe my fuel pump or coolant temp sensor was shot when I'd have to keep turning it over to get her running, but just for mindsake I put the oem coil from my parts car back in and she started right up. I paid an extra $15.00 and got the Accel cause I thought it would outlast any oem coil and advertised smoother acceleration. It was fine for the first few days. I know the Fiero Store won't take it back cause I painted the metal shroud around it with high heat black to keep it from rusting. Oh well, lesson learned. Just thought I should share so you'd stay away from them.||||reg||13
Z||000001||Gokart||04-29-2000||06:43 AM||||Well, hold on to your hats! Went out to start her up this this morn and she won't turn over with my back up coil in. Man, this has not been my week <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> It cranks fine and I can hear the fuel pump turn on but it won't kick in! Cold start valve? Fuel pump relay? Distributor module? Was reading in my factory service manual that it could be a loose ignition coil ground? Then how could she started up last night if it needs to be grounded and I just put the connections on my back up coil and it wasn't mounted in? Maybe cause I painted the metal shrouds on them to keep them from getting rusted? Where should I start? Thank God it's Saturday so I'm not going to work.||||reg||5
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||04-29-2000||09:02 AM||||Gokart, I had a similar problem when I installed my Accel coil. My car bucked with a poor idle. I thought that was strange. But found out that I had to get new heavyduty plug wires to handle the load of the coil. After install all is well.     ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||04-29-2000||11:54 AM||||Gokart when I installed my MSD I had no problems at all, but I did do a complete tune up at the same time and used ACCEL 300+ wires<BR>I have not and any problems, it's been in now for about 7-8 months within that time I did replace the dist., but it is still fine!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||04-30-2000||05:45 AM||||Got some 8.8mm wires on order and they'll be in on Teusday. Hope this solves my starting problem. I don't feel the need to change the plugs though. I only got about 10,000 miles on these Bosch platinum+4's and there good for 50,000+.||||reg||2
Z||000005||batboy||04-30-2000||08:50 AM||||Sorry to burst your bubble, but it probably not the coil or the plug wires (remember, same intermittant problem using both coils). It could be a shorted or grounded electrical wire, or malfunctioning ignition module or ignition pickup coil (inside distributor), or a lot of things. Ogre, give this man some ideas about where to check!||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||04-30-2000||08:53 AM||||I reread your post, try scraping a little paint off the bracket so the bolt head contacts metal, maybe you lost your ground when you painted the bracket.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gokart||04-30-2000||08:43 PM||||I did that to be on the safe side, but I had a feeling it wouldn't make a difference cause when I plugged in my back-up coil I didn't even have it mounted in and it started! How can this be if it needs to be "grounded"? Maybe it grounds itself with the two connectors that go in it. Tested and swapped distributor modules with the help of Cobra today, both passed and knowing again it couldn't be as well with the distributor coil cause they are less than 6 months old! I can hear the fuel pump turn on so that eliminates the relay. Still starts awful at times and I have a feeling she might leave me stranded somewhere. I'm getting the wires on Teusday and I'm hoping there's a difference. Could be a short somewhere but maybe a plug wire or two are cooked? Will let you know...Thanks, Marc<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 04-30-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000008||theogre||04-30-2000||08:59 PM||||When exposed coil frames are grounded it mostly coincidental. GM HEI uses esentially the same coil wether it's on a bracket or in the cap. At most grounding the frame knocks down RFI. The frame forms no part of the coils electrical circuits.<P>From my espeirience with my Accell DIS coils, I'm guessing the HEI coil is drawing more current thru the PIM. This will heat up the PIM. The exact result is hard to predict. Usually as soon as the PIM cools on my DIS brick it runs fine. On the smaller HEI PIM you may have damaged it.<P>in your case you put the OEM coil back and had problems. You swaped PIMs and had the same problem. You by now have changed the wires and are still having problems? <P>pull the spark plugs and check them just to make sure, but my guess it they are fine. Altho it is psoible the increased coil current cooked them. <P>check the cap and rotor really carefully. It's not at all uncommon for hot coils to cook the center contact out.<P>has the distributor pickup been changed? ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Gokart||05-01-2000||09:38 PM||||Yes Ogre, the pick up is only 6 months old. I replaced the distributor last fall. Changed the cap and rotor then too and they look good. My new wires will be in tomorrow, but I wonder if I should even put them in now if the PIM is damaged on the new Accel coil? Could this now damaged the new wires? I'll pull the plugs and take a look this weekend. I'm guessing they'll be fine too cause their Bosch platinum+4 with only 10,000 miles on them...TKS||||reg||1
Z||000010||Gokart||05-03-2000||06:15 AM||||Can't say the wires solved the starting problems I was having because they went away Sunday when Cobra and I were checking different things, but these 8.8 Accel wires definatly improved performance! Cleared the code 32 from the ecm and that hasn't come back either! Maybe it was that time of the month for my GT <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
